.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 17, 2026
.Dt NULLFS 5
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm nullfs
.Nd a directory seen again at another place
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm mount_nullfs
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Ar target node
.Nm mount
.Fl t Cm nullfs
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Ar target node
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A
.Nm
mount shows the directory
.Ar target ,
of any mounted file system, at
.Ar node :
the same files, which a change made through either path changes in both.
Linux calls this a bind mount
.Pq Nm mount Fl -bind .
.Pp
The mount has a device number
.Pq Fa st_dev
of its own; a file keeps its serial number.
Its options are its own, whatever the file system below has:
.Cm ro
makes it read-only
.Pq Er EROFS ,
.Cm nosuid
ignores set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits and
.Cm noexec
runs nothing from it, all only through
.Ar node .
A file can't be linked or renamed between it and another mount, the
.Ar target Ns 's
own included
.Pq Er EXDEV .
.Pp
While it is mounted, the file system holding
.Ar target
can't be unmounted
.Pq Er EBUSY ,
except by force, after which the
.Nm
mount shows nothing.
.Pp
.Ar node Ns 's
.Pa ..
is the directory
.Ar node
is in, not
.Ar target Ns 's
parent.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The sources, read-only, inside a
.Xr chroot 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount_nullfs -o ro /usr/src /jail/usr/src
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr mount 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
appeared in 4.4BSD.
In OxideBSD it was a part of oxfs, showing only oxfs's directories, until it became a file
system type of its own over any other in 0.3.0.
